---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs"
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import { Callout, CalloutContent, CalloutIcon } from "opui-css/astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import BlockShuffle from "../../components/Release/BlockShuffle.astro"
import Blueprint from "../../components/Release/Blueprint.astro"
import ContrastLab from "../../components/Release/ContrastLab.astro"
import Hero from "../../components/Release/Hero.astro"
import NewComponents from "../../components/Release/NewComponents.astro"
import Numbers from "../../components/Release/Numbers.astro"
import Ratchets from "../../components/Release/Ratchets.astro"
import Renames from "../../components/Release/Renames.astro"
import Shells from "../../components/Release/Shells.astro"
import SupportMatrix from "../../components/Release/SupportMatrix.astro"
import Takeover from "../../components/Release/Takeover.astro"
import ThemeDice from "../../components/Release/ThemeDice.astro"
import TitleCard from "../../components/Release/TitleCard.astro"
import TokenTiers from "../../components/TokenTiers.astro"
import ButtonBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/ButtonBuild.astro"
import { allBlocks, blockGroups } from "../../utils/blocks"
import { addedComponents } from "../../utils/changelog"
import { releaseVersion } from "../../utils/release"

const newComponents = addedComponents(
  readFileSync("packages/opui/CHANGELOG.md", "utf8"),
  releaseVersion,
)
---

<TechniquePost slug="release-6">
  <Callout class="release-tldr" severity="info">
    <CalloutIcon />
    <CalloutContent>
      <p><strong>TL;DR</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          What is in 6.0: React and Svelte, {newComponents.length} new components
          and the Layout classes, a token system with four example themes to prove
          it, blocks, the Theme builder and a lot more docs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Why a major: 105 breaking entries, the first major since 5.0, most of
          them renames.
        </li>
        <li>
          What stayed: CSS first, HTML as a first-class target, feature first.
        </li>
        <li>
          Where to go next: <DocLink href="/guide/migrating">Migrating</DocLink>
          for the mechanics, this post for the reasons.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </CalloutContent>
  </Callout>

  <TitleCard />

  <p>
    When the project started everything was written by hand. The focus was to be
    the component library that uses modern CSS and HTML that didn't seem to
    exist yet. In short, I wanted to build that library, learn all the cool
    tricks and techniques and ultimately pass them on. Not that I counted on
    having a massive user base, but inspiring at least one person was a win and
    good enough for me. I really liked the shadcn model - they did the hard
    work, you get to grab everything and make it your own, so a key
    consideration from the start was to have very pragmatic and readable CSS
    that any user at any level could grasp and then modify.
  </p>

  <p>That was then.</p>

  <p>
    Well I mean you can still do all these things (don't worry), but since AI
    has gotten so good that writing anything by hand has become unnecessary, the
    core concepts stayed but the scope changed. I could think bigger in terms of
    the library's scope and architecture. In 6.0, the CSS has become less
    readable for sure, but with the added benefit of the features that the new
    design system and layer architecture provided. I always had the 6.0
    structure in mind when I started out the project, but it felt like it
    wouldn't be very approachable for people and I had worries about maintaining
    that on a hobby level.
  </p>

  <Blueprint state="sketch" />

  <p>
    Now with a chance to start fresh, I wanted to implement my initial vision.
    Wouldn't it be cool to have one CSS codebase that was flexible and thought
    through enough to handle almost any style - shadcn, Material design,
    Bootstrap (I know, but it has charm), some wacky brutalist experiment, you
    name it! If I could make that work, I could be pretty certain it would be
    flexible enough that anyone else could probably customize it to their needs
    as well.
  </p>

  <p>
    After deciding on this direction, I still wanted a way for people to pick up
    on the CSS tricks, so I went all in on documentation. Not only better
    component docs, but also a whole new blog dedicated to explaining the inner
    workings and design decisions behind each component. Now, I know very few
    people will read it, I know, I know, but maybe someone will point their LLM
    to it and it will find it useful. I don't know, somehow I'd like to move the
    needle regardless of how little. If nothing else I'm having a lot of fun
    doing this anyhow.
  </p>

  <p>Enough yapping, here's what I'd like to highlight for 6.0!</p>

  <Hero />

  <section id="design-system">
    <h2>It's a proper design system now</h2>
    <p>
      Or at least I've tried. I'm no Brad Frost or Dave Rupert or whatever, but
      I've been around and hopefully picked up a thing or two along the way. In
      case I haven't, that's why I'm throwing all these example themes on the
      library as a stress test. CSS variable/token tiering and more robust
      architecture are two of my absolute favorite additions to 6.0. This
      re-architecture also exposed a lot of weak points in the previous design,
      so I was kind of forced to go through all components and update them.
    </p>
    <p>Don't take my word for it. Pick a theme and this whole post wears it.</p>
    <Takeover />

    <h3 id="example-themes">Four example themes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Bootstrap, Brutalist, M3 and Shadcn: what each one proves about the
        token model.
      </li>
      <li>
        All colors in OKLCH; the contrast test lists each theme's known
        exceptions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Hooks a theme can reach: <code>--ui-control-accent</code>,
        <code>--ui-selected</code>, <code>--ui-button-hover: layer</code>,
        <code>--ui-border-color-raised</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="tokens-tiers">Token tiers</h3>
    <p>
      Read a column top to bottom: a knob the theme sets, the role a component
      reads, the private property the component sets, the CSS property it ends
      up on.
    </p>
    <TokenTiers />
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every token prefixed <code>--ui-</code>, and the rename table in
        <DocLink href="/guide/migrating#theme-and-tokens"
          >Theme and tokens</DocLink
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        Three tiers, knobs then roles then hooks, and which one to touch for
        what (<DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens">Theme tokens</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Derived roles declared on
        <code>:where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more)</code>, so a
        <code>.ui-palette</code> subtree with an inline knob re-themes itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        The layers: <code>theme.palette</code>, <code>theme.base</code>,
        <code>theme.custom</code>, <code>theme.severity</code>, and why severity
        sits above your theme.
      </li>
      <li>
        Design scales: space <code>--ui-space-1</code> to
        <code>--ui-space-8</code> times <code>--ui-space-scale</code>,
        <code>--ui-density</code>, the type scale
        <code>--ui-font-size-100</code> to <code>--ui-font-size-900</code>,
        motion times <code>--ui-motion</code>, one
        <code>--ui-shadow-color</code> for both schemes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open Props is optional; <code>theme.css</code> reads nothing from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>tokens.json</code> in the W3C design tokens format, with tiers, dark
        and more-contrast modes, and the promised contrast per token.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="contrast-guarantee">Contrast as a guarantee</h3>
    <ContrastLab />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-contrast: more</code> tightens 26 knobs, lightness, strength, alpha
        and widths, and never sets a color directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>-text</code> roles promise 4.5:1 on every surface; the promise is
        recorded in <code>tokens.json</code> and tested.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-on-primary</code> picked from the fill's luminance with relative
        color.
      </li>
      <li>Focus ring black or white by surface lightness.</li>
      <li>
        The sweep: hue 0 to 360 in steps of 10, normal and more contrast, light
        and dark, 1036 pairs, none below the promise. The closest call is
        on-primary text at hue 190 in light mode, 4.77:1.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="new-components">
    <h2>Loads of new components</h2>
    <p>
      {newComponents.length} of them, plus the Layout classes. When HTML can't do
      the job on its own, a tiny custom element does it, the same one for every framework
      and for plain HTML.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/avatar#grouped">AvatarGroup</DocLink>:
        overlapping avatars that make room for the focus ring.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/carousel">Carousel</DocLink>: scroll snap,
        with the buttons and markers generated by CSS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/copy">Copy</DocLink>: copies a field or a
        snippet with one invoker command.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/layout">Layout</DocLink>: rows, grids and
        columns that wrap without breakpoints.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/menu">Menu</DocLink>: a popover with
        submenus, opened with invoker commands.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/message">Message</DocLink>: chat bubbles that
        group themselves into runs with <code>:has()</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/number-field">NumberField</DocLink>: decrease
        and increase buttons around a number input.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/one-time-code">OneTimeCode</DocLink>: one
        input, drawn as a box per character.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/rating">Rating</DocLink>: stars from one
        mask, as a score or as input.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox#select-all">SelectAll</DocLink>: one
        checkbox that checks the rest of its group.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/table#sorting">SortableTable</DocLink>: press
        a header to sort the rows.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/stat">Stat</DocLink>: the big number on a
        dashboard.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/stepper">Stepper</DocLink>: the steps of a
        flow such as a checkout.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/timeline">Timeline</DocLink>: dated events,
        lined up with subgrid.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/toast">Toaster</DocLink>: toasts from a
        button, without a line of your own JavaScript.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      And the smaller ones: a <DocLink
        href="/components/text-field#password-toggle">password toggle</DocLink
      > and a <DocLink href="/components/text-field#character-count"
        >character count</DocLink
      > on TextField, <DocLink href="/components/divider#content"
        >content in a Divider</DocLink
      >, and x-small and large sizes for every field. Go on, poke at a few:
    </p>
    <NewComponents />
  </section>

  <section id="docs">
    <h2>So much cool stuff in the docs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Anatomy diagrams that name every part of a component and the class that
        styles it.
      </li>
      <li>
        API tables built from the same data the tests check, and the
        <DocLink href="/api">API index</DocLink> with every component on one page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Browser support on every component page, with Baseline status, and a
        <DocLink href="/guide/browser-support">guide</DocLink> to the compat layer.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every page in HTML, Astro, React, Svelte and Vue, with the examples and
        the API of the framework you picked.
      </li>
      <li>
        Markdown for your agent: a <code>.md</code> copy of every page,
        <code>llms.txt</code> per framework, and <code>npx opui-css skill</code>
        to drop a skill for opui into your project.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <DocLink href="/blog">blog</DocLink>: a post per technique, and an
        <DocLink href="/blog/under-the-hood">Under the hood</DocLink> build-up for
        most components.
      </li>
      <li>
        What's new and Migrating from 5.x notes at the top of each component
        page.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The build-ups are my answer to the less readable CSS. Here's the Button,
      one rule at a time:
    </p>
    <ButtonBuild />
  </section>

  <section id="blocks">
    <h2>Blocks!</h2>
    <p>
      I know no one will probably use a single one of them. AI will barf out
      these blocks in five seconds - it's why Tailwind Plus (or whatever it's
      called) isn't a thing anymore. But if no one uses them, they will at least
      serve as a showcase of what can be done and how you can work with OPUI.
      Also a great way to find new components to add back to the library. For
      each block I add, I hope to demonstrate the possibilities but also find
      ways to improve the library itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are {allBlocks.length} of them in {blockGroups.length} categories, plain
      HTML and CSS, the same in every framework.
    </p>
    <BlockShuffle />
  </section>

  <section id="theme-builder">
    <h2>Theme builder</h2>
    <p>This one is exciting!</p>
    <p>
      Not only do I really get to use OPUI and stretch its legs here, but for
      users it provides a way to easily customize their themes without having to
      learn every token and variable and how they interact.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Export to <code>theme.css</code> or <code>tokens.json</code>.</li>
      <li>
        Import a theme's CSS, a list of colors, or an image to take the brand
        color from.
      </li>
      <li>Moods, and a shuffle that leaves the settings you locked alone.</li>
      <li>
        Preview on components, states, blocks or this whole site, in light, dark
        and more contrast, with a contrast report.
      </li>
      <li>
        Save up to 50 themes in the browser, undo and redo, and share a theme as
        a link.
      </li>
      <li>
        Start from the default, an example theme or one of the gallery themes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Roll one below. If you like it, take it with you to the builder.</p>
    <ThemeDice />
  </section>

  <section id="the-css-is-the-component">
    <h2>The CSS is the component</h2>
    <Shells />
    <ul>
      <li>
        The core idea: a component is its stylesheet and the native HTML it
        styles. Popovers, invokers, anchor positioning, scroll-driven fills and
        <code>:has()</code> carry the behavior.
      </li>
      <li>
        A framework layer is a binding: props to attributes and classes, slots
        to parts, events to native events. About 2,500 lines each.
      </li>
      <li>
        Behavior HTML cannot give lives in custom elements, shared by every
        framework and by plain HTML.
      </li>
      <li>
        Why React and Svelte could land in one release, and what each binding
        adds: React 19 with Server Components by default and
        <code>"use client"</code> only on stateful components; Svelte 5 with
        <code>bind:value</code>, <code>bind:checked</code> and
        <code>bind:group</code>; Astro and Vue with parts instead of slots.
      </li>
      <li>
        The parity test keeps the shells honest: every example rendered in every
        framework and compared against Astro, accepted drift in a
        <code>.diff</code> ledger that only shrinks, 22 alignment fixes in this release.
      </li>
      <li>
        What the idea costs: a new feature needs one engine to ship it, so the
        browser support section is the other half of this one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="one-naming-model">
    <h2>Slots become parts, props get one naming model</h2>
    <Renames />
    <ul>
      <li>
        Slots become parts: CardHeader, CardContent, CardActions, DialogTitle,
        DialogFooter, AnchorContent, TooltipContent, AccordionSummary.
      </li>
      <li>
        Props named after the native attribute or the CSS they set:
        <code>positionArea</code>, <code>checked</code>,
        <code>orientation</code>, <code>start</code> and <code>end</code>,
        <code>size="small"</code>, <code>type="button"</code> by default.
      </li>
      <li>
        Private custom properties follow one shape: <code>--_accent</code>,
        <code>--_text-color</code>, <code>--_bg-color</code>,
        <code>--_size</code>, <code>--_radius</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The naming model as a written rule in the components guide, enforced by
        a unit test.
      </li>
      <li>Removed: IconButton (Button does it), ClassicSelect, Toast.</li>
      <li>
        The explorer above holds the 119 renames the migration guide documents
        as pairs; structural changes (slots to parts) are in the guide only.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="platform-did-the-work">
    <h2>The platform did the work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/carousel">Carousel</DocLink> with CSS scroll buttons
        and markers (<DocLink href="/blog/carousel-css-only">Blog</DocLink>), <DocLink
          href="/components/menu">Menu</DocLink
        > as a popover with submenus, anchored without <code>anchor-name</code> (<DocLink
          href="/blog/menu-popover-anchor">Blog</DocLink
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        Invoker commands for Dialog, Drawer and Menu (<DocLink
          href="/blog/dialog-closedby">Blog</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/blog/drawer-starting-style">Blog</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>interestfor</code> for Tooltip and Anchor, and the
        <code>id</code> that is now yours to pass (<DocLink
          href="/blog/tooltip-interest-invokers">Blog</DocLink
        >, <DocLink href="/blog/anchor-hover-cards">Blog</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Scroll-driven Range fill (<DocLink href="/blog/range-tick-marks"
          >Blog</DocLink
        >), scroll-state shadows on Dialog, Drawer and sticky Table headers (<DocLink
          href="/blog/table-scroll-state">Blog</DocLink
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        Select on <code>appearance: base-select</code> with
        <code>autoFit</code> (<DocLink href="/blog/select-base-select"
          >Blog</DocLink
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        Fluid headings with <code>progress()</code> (<DocLink
          href="/blog/fluid-type-progress">Blog</DocLink
        >), rhythm snapped with <code>round()</code> (<DocLink
          href="/blog/vertical-rhythm-round">Blog</DocLink
        >), the <code>components.prose</code> layer.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tabs announced as a radio group (<DocLink
          href="/blog/tabs-radio-buttons">Blog</DocLink
        >), <code>aria-invalid</code> replacing <code>data-invalid</code>
        (<DocLink href="/blog/text-field-user-invalid">Blog</DocLink>),
        forced-colors fixes across the board.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="browser-support">
    <h2>Browser support, honestly</h2>
    <SupportMatrix />
    <ul>
      <li>Feature first: one engine shipping is enough.</li>
      <li>
        Expected failures per engine, each with a plain-HTML repro, removed the
        day the engine catches up.
      </li>
      <li>
        The compat layer and what it covers today (<DocLink
          href="/guide/browser-support">Browser support</DocLink
        >).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>opui.no-compat.css</code> and
        <code>opui.baseline-&lt;year&gt;.css</code>: pick by audience.
      </li>
      <li>
        Known gaps to name: iOS checked by hand, logical
        <code>overflow-inline</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="new-by-hand">
    <h2>The new "by hand"</h2>
    <Ratchets />
    <ul>
      <li>
        Instead of keeping every detail in your head, lean on tools and scripts
        to harden the library, and spend the attention on architecture and the
        big picture.
      </li>
      <li>
        What hardens it: released tokens and released API ledgers, parity drift,
        the a11y violations ledger, contrast promises, ratchets that only
        shrink.
      </li>
      <li>
        The kind of bug they catch: palette knobs declared twice, with a copy
        that disagreed and never applied.
      </li>
      <li>
        The exception is adding something new: that is where the fun is,
        micro-managing and directing every detail carefully.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="where-it-landed">
    <h2>Where it landed</h2>
    <Blueprint state="built" />
    <Numbers />
    <ul>
      <li>
        The numbers above are computed at build time from the changelog, the
        docs and the package. The commit count needs a full clone.
      </li>
      <li>
        Themes overriding the severity palette until the
        <code>theme.severity</code> layer.
      </li>
      <li>
        The compat layer: one file per component, every rule behind
        <code>@supports not</code> or an engine gate with a plain-HTML repro.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="migrating">
    <h2>Migrating</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Import order: <code>layers.css</code> first; <code>choice.css</code>,
        <code>utils.css</code> and <code>compat.css</code> with single-file imports.
      </li>
      <li>
        Types from the framework entry; <code>types.vue-props.ts</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <a href="#one-naming-model">rename explorer</a> for props, classes, tokens
        and private properties.
      </li>
      <li>
        Visual shifts to expect: rich text spacing and heading sizes, the Tabs
        look, control sizes in rem, Radio at 20px, darker dark-mode shadows.
      </li>
      <li>
        Everything else, per component, in
        <DocLink href="/guide/migrating">Migrating</DocLink>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>

<style>
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      section > h2,
      section > :global(:not(h2, #takeover)) {
        animation: release-reveal linear backwards;
        animation-range: entry 0% entry 30%;
        animation-timeline: view();
      }
    }
  }

  @keyframes release-reveal {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      translate: 0 1rem;
    }

    to {
      opacity: 1;
      translate: none;
    }
  }
</style>
